nja who  i just immediately fell in love with you  say this is a relatable story even to  people who are not of korean descent why  absolutely  so many people have come up to me  through the making of this show for the  four years  people from all backgrounds all ages and  they talk about i just want you to know  this reminds me of my grandmother she  came over from germany she came over  from ghana she came over and then you  just realize everyone has a sunja in  their lives all of us  um and that's what this show has done  it's really started that conversation  with so many people so many tears yes  yes lots of tier and really tugs at the  heartstrings also it's so celebratory  too yes i want people you drew me in  with the open of the show your decision  the decision that you made to have such  a celebratory open because i to be  honest i didn't even know what pachinko  was i didn't know if it was a person i  didn't know if it was a place you knew  huh yeah well i mean i spent some time  in japan um in korea but but also i read  the book my wife and i read the book by  minjin lee and so but but tell everyone  what what did you know what pachinko is  no no i had no idea so pachinko is this  arcade-like game it's like a pinball  machine straight up and it's a game of  chance balls go flying in the air and  you either win or lose you have no  control over it and it's a great  metaphor for the show as well right  and you say the show is it's it's a  universal story about humanity now when  we read the book we thought to ourselves  this has got to be turned into a movie  or a series i'm a huge fan of  yeah it's a wonderful book you've made  some changes but the universal theme of  that humanity is inherent throughout the  entirety of it good  i mean because the book was this  staggering achievement in thinking about  the adaptation to screen the question  was how do we tell this story  in the cinematic form which is so  different from the novel form and the  thing i was most interested was that  conversation between generations because  it's my story yeah my parents came to  this country i was an immigrant it's a  it's the story that i really latched on  to because it was the most personal you  said when you grew up you didn't have  access to filmmaking so tell us a little  bit about a thousand miles yeah  yeah it's your program yeah or aspiring  writers and i give ucp and universal so  much credit for really putting their  mouth and their checkbook where you know  in terms for this program but growing up  in towson maryland and wanting to be a  filmmaker i always say  hollywood felt like going to the moon it  might as well be the moon right yeah and  i just don't want that  sense of struggle to i would love it if  people had a better opening your program  is called a thousand miles and it's  based on this chinese proverb that the  road to a thousand miles starts with one  single step and i just i love that idea  can i just say you look pretty good  sitting on top of the moon  one final review pachinko is a triumph  of visual storytelling that goes in its  own direction and finds a destination  that leaves you absolutely floored and  how masterfully it executes its vision  so bravo bravo  thank you so much for joining us thank  you for having me we appreciate you you  can watch pachinko starting today on  apple tv plus
 

